The topological entropy is a fundamental invariant of dynamical systems, measuring the exponential growth rate of the number of distinguishable orbits. For a continuous map $f\colon X \to X$ on a compact metric space $(X,d)$, the topological entropy $h_{\mathrm{top}}(f)$ is defined as follows. For $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $\varepsilon > 0$, a subset $S \subset X$ is called $(n,\varepsilon)$-separated if for any distinct $x,y \in S$ there exists $0 \le k < n$ such that $d(f^k(x), f^k(y)) > \varepsilon$. Let $N(n,\varepsilon)$ denote the maximal cardinality of an $(n,\varepsilon)$-separated set. Then

\begin{equation}
    h_{\mathrm{top}}(f) = \lim_{\varepsilon \to 0} \limsup_{n \to \infty} \frac{1}{n} \log N(n,\varepsilon).
\end{equation}

The topological entropy is invariant under topological conjugacy, and it is well known that $h_{\mathrm{top}}$ is lower semi-continuous in various settings.

